# Answer key for t2_depth.sql - B1 core SELECT, depth.
#
# WRITTEN FROM SOURCE BEFORE THE ANALYSER WAS RUN OVER THIS FILE.
#
# The test is that every fact below is the SAME fact T1 would give for the construct at
# the top level. Renaming and nesting change no edge; where they appear to, that is the
# finding.
#
# Influence COMPOSES along a path: a GROUP BY or window several levels down influences
# whatever the aggregated or windowed value finally lands in.
#
# FLAGGED, NOT LABELLED: S5's `WHERE g.gset = 1` filters on GROUPING(), i.e. on the
# grouping-set structure rather than on any column's data. No convention exists; nothing
# is labelled or forbidden for it (same treatment as T1 S10a).

@ 23 S1 alias chain, five levels
value staging.customers.id -> mart.top_customers.customer_id identity
value staging.customers.full_name -> mart.top_customers.full_name identity
forbid value staging.customers.id -> mart.top_customers.customer_id derived | five renames compute nothing; the edge stays identity

@ 51 S2 window four levels down
value core.fct_order_line.product_key -> mart.product_performance.product_id identity
value core.fct_order_line.order_date -> mart.product_performance.period_month identity
value core.fct_order_line.net_amount -> mart.product_performance.running_revenue aggregated
influence core.fct_order_line.product_key, core.fct_order_line.order_date -> mart.product_performance.running_revenue
filter core.fct_order_line.status -> mart.product_performance

@ 84 S3 re-aggregation, two GROUP BYs on one path
value core.fct_order_line.store_id -> mart.sales_daily.store_id identity
value core.fct_order_line.net_amount -> mart.sales_daily.avg_order_value aggregated
value core.fct_order_line.order_id -> mart.sales_daily.orders aggregated
influence core.fct_order_line.store_id, core.fct_order_line.order_id -> mart.sales_daily.avg_order_value
note store_id groups at both levels; order_id only at the inner one, where it decides what each order_total is.
influence core.fct_order_line.store_id -> mart.sales_daily.orders
note COUNT(po.oid) counts the inner grouping key; only the OUTER grouping is applied to an aggregate on this path.

@ 113 S4 HAVING three levels down, WHERE over an aggregate at the top
value staging.orders.customer_id -> mart.customer_summary.customer_id identity
value staging.orders.id -> mart.customer_summary.lifetime_orders aggregated
value staging.orders.amount -> mart.customer_summary.lifetime_value aggregated
influence staging.orders.customer_id -> mart.customer_summary.lifetime_orders, mart.customer_summary.lifetime_value
filter staging.orders.status -> mart.customer_summary
filter staging.orders.amount, staging.orders.id -> mart.customer_summary post
note amount: HAVING SUM(amount). id: the outer `WHERE t.n_orders >= 3` filters COUNT(o.id), so it acts after aggregation (D-6).

@ 141 S5 ROLLUP inner, GROUPING() filter outer
value core.dim_customer.region -> mart.kpi_rollup.region identity
value core.dim_customer.segment -> mart.kpi_rollup.segment identity
value core.fct_order_line.net_amount -> mart.kpi_rollup.revenue aggregated
influence core.dim_customer.region, core.dim_customer.segment -> mart.kpi_rollup.revenue
filter core.dim_customer.is_current -> mart.kpi_rollup

@ 163 S6 a join inside a derived table, joined again outside
value staging.orders.order_ts -> mart.channel_mix.period_month derived
value staging.orders.channel -> mart.channel_mix.channel identity
value core.dim_store.region -> mart.channel_mix.region identity
value staging.order_lines.quantity, staging.order_lines.unit_price, staging.fx_rates.rate_to_usd -> mart.channel_mix.revenue derived
forbid filter staging.fx_rates.rate_date -> mart.channel_mix | an ON clause is structural (D-5), even when its operand was computed one level down
forbid filter staging.orders.currency -> mart.channel_mix | an ON clause is structural (D-5), even when its operand was computed one level down

@ 191 S7 aggregate FILTER three levels down
value core.fct_shipment.carrier_id -> mart.carrier_scorecard.carrier_id identity
value core.fct_shipment.transit_days, core.fct_shipment.delivered_date, core.fct_shipment.promised_date -> mart.carrier_scorecard.on_time_pct aggregated
influence core.fct_shipment.carrier_id -> mart.carrier_scorecard.on_time_pct
forbid filter core.fct_shipment.delivered_date -> mart.carrier_scorecard | FILTER (WHERE) changes one aggregate's value, at any depth (D-10)

@ 211 S8 DISTINCT ON two levels down, feeding an aggregate
value staging.orders.currency -> mart.channel_mix.channel identity
value staging.orders.customer_id -> mart.channel_mix.orders aggregated
value staging.orders.amount -> mart.channel_mix.revenue aggregated
influence staging.orders.currency -> mart.channel_mix.orders, mart.channel_mix.revenue
filter staging.orders.customer_id, staging.orders.updated_at -> mart.channel_mix
note D-9: the DISTINCT ON key and its tie-breaker decide which order per customer survives into the aggregate.

@ 232 S9 a predicate five levels down, never projected
value staging.payments.order_id -> mart.top_customers.customer_id identity
value staging.payments.amount -> mart.top_customers.revenue identity
filter staging.payments.method, staging.payments.currency -> mart.top_customers

@ 256 S10 a column carried up and dropped
value staging.inventory_snapshots.snapshot_date -> mart.sales_daily.sale_date identity
value staging.inventory_snapshots.on_hand -> mart.sales_daily.units identity
forbid value staging.inventory_snapshots.reserved -> mart.sales_daily.units | projected at depth 3 and 2 as unused_cost, never read by the level that writes
forbid filter staging.inventory_snapshots.reserved -> mart.sales_daily | carried, never compared to anything

@ 273 S11 a named window two levels down, used twice
value core.fct_order_line.order_line_id -> mart.product_performance.product_id identity
value core.fct_order_line.net_amount -> mart.product_performance.prev_month_revenue derived
value core.fct_order_line.quantity -> mart.product_performance.growth_pct derived
influence core.fct_order_line.store_id, core.fct_order_line.order_date -> mart.product_performance.prev_month_revenue, mart.product_performance.growth_pct

@ 290 S12 three sibling CTEs at three grains
value core.fct_order_line.customer_key -> mart.customer_summary.customer_id identity
value core.fct_order_line.order_date -> mart.customer_summary.last_order_date identity
value core.fct_order_line.net_amount -> mart.customer_summary.lifetime_value aggregated
value core.fct_order_line.order_id -> mart.customer_summary.lifetime_orders aggregated
value core.fct_order_line.unit_price -> mart.customer_summary.avg_basket aggregated
influence core.fct_order_line.customer_key -> mart.customer_summary.lifetime_value
influence core.fct_order_line.order_date -> mart.customer_summary.lifetime_orders
influence core.fct_order_line.customer_key, core.fct_order_line.order_date -> mart.customer_summary.avg_basket
forbid influence core.fct_order_line.order_date -> mart.customer_summary.lifetime_value | by_customer groups by customer_key only; order_date is by_day's grouping
forbid influence core.fct_order_line.customer_key -> mart.customer_summary.lifetime_orders | by_day groups by order_date only; customer_key is by_customer's grouping

@ 326 S13 a window over an aggregate three levels below it
value core.fct_order_line.order_date -> mart.sales_daily.sale_date identity
value core.dim_store.region -> mart.sales_daily.region identity
value core.fct_order_line.gross_amount -> mart.sales_daily.gross_amount aggregated
influence core.fct_order_line.store_id, core.fct_order_line.order_date -> mart.sales_daily.gross_amount
influence core.dim_store.region, core.fct_order_line.order_date, core.fct_order_line.gross_amount, core.fct_order_line.store_id -> mart.sales_daily.rank_in_region
note region and order_date partition the rank; gross_amount orders it through the SUM; store_id and order_date decided what each SUM is.
forbid value core.fct_order_line.gross_amount -> mart.sales_daily.rank_in_region | RANK's ORDER BY decides the rank; no part of the sum is in it (D-4)
